WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.653
When Marc reached the platform, the board already said 'cancelled' next to the 11:12 p.m. train. He read it three times, as if the letters might change their mind. The next train left at 5:48. He had a job interview in Rennes at nine, and his phone had only eight per cent battery left.

00:00:19.903 --> 00:00:32.282
In the waiting room there was only an old lady, sitting very upright, a canvas bag on her knees. She was knitting. Marc sat down two chairs away from her, sighing louder than he would have liked.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:51.232
'Going far?' she asked without looking up. He explained about the interview, the cancelled train, the battery. She nodded, rummaged in her bag and took out a thermos of coffee and a charger. 'My grandson makes me take it everywhere. I don't even know how to use it.'

00:00:51.482 --> 00:01:11.497
They talked for a good part of the night. She had been a railway worker for thirty-five years; she knew every line in the region and told stories of strikes, storms, and the famous passengers she claimed to have met. Around four, Marc realised he had not thought about his interview for a long time, and that he was no longer afraid.

00:01:11.747 --> 00:01:30.357
At 5:48 the train pulled in. When Marc turned to say goodbye, the old lady was already putting her knitting away. 'Go on,' she said. 'I'll wait for the next one. I'm in no hurry.' It was only on the train that he realised she had never told him where she was going.
